Mild allergic conjunctivitis causing bilateral watery and itchy eyes without photophobia
Minor Ailments and Upsets
Intense bilateral ocular pruritus, Watery epiphora, Diffuse conjunctival hyperemia

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MAU-014
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of bilateral red, weeping eyes requiring assessment to rule out infectious keratitis or acute angle-closure glaucoma.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted sudden onset of intensely red and watery eyes. Urgent physical assessment was strictly required prior to the next in-hours period to evaluate pupillary reflexes, assess for corneal involvement, and exclude sight-threatening conditions such as acute angle-closure glaucoma or bacterial keratitis.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My eyes are completely red, watering constantly, and itch uncontrollably.
History of Present Illness: Patient describes sudden onset of intense bilateral ocular pruritus starting a few hours ago after outdoor yard work. Reports profuse watery non-purulent epiphora. Notes concomitant mild allergic rhinitis symptoms (sneezing). Complete absence of photophobia, foreign body sensation, or deep aching eye pain. Denies any change in visual acuity.
Functional Status: Fully independent, vision grossly intact.
Social/Safety Concerns: Intense rubbing of eyes risks secondary corneal abrasion.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 120/78 | HR: 86 | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Alert, frequently wiping eyes, sneezing intermittently.
Physical Exam: Ocular examination reveals mild diffuse conjunctival hyperemia and chemosis (swelling of the bulbar conjunctiva). Normal uncorrected visual acuity to gross testing. Normal pupillary light reflexes bilaterally. Complete absence of photophobia or ciliary flush. No purulent discharge. Diagnostic triad confirmed: intense bilateral ocular pruritus, watery epiphora, and diffuse conjunctival hyperemia.
Environmental Observations: Windows open, high ambient pollen count visible outdoors.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient taking no regular ophthalmic drops.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Mild allergic conjunctivitis causing bilateral watery and itchy eyes without photophobia - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Acute Angle-Closure Glaucoma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Bilateral presentation, normal pupillary light reflexes, and absence of deep aching eye pain or halos around lights.

Bacterial Conjunctivitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Bilateral simultaneous onset, intense pruritus rather than grittiness, and complete absence of purulent or mucopurulent discharge.

Corneal Abrasion - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Bilateral presentation, absence of focal foreign body sensation, and lack of photophobia.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The sudden development of bilateral hyperemic, weeping eyes shares initial features with severe infectious or sight-threatening ocular pathology. An urgent face-to-face evaluation was mandatory to check visual acuity, assess pupillary reactivity, and rule out ciliary flush or corneal compromise. The clinical confirmation of an acute IgE-mediated allergic response safely averted unnecessary escalation to the emergency department, satisfying the requirement for an immediate after-hours assessment to prevent deterioration (e.g., from severe eye rubbing leading to abrasion).

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescription for topical ophthalmic antihistamine/mast cell stabilizer drops (e.g., olopatadine or ketotifen). Recommendation for oral non-sedating antihistamine to manage concurrent rhinitis. Application of cold compresses over closed eyelids.
Positive Behavioral Change: Instill chilled artificial tears whenever the urge to rub the eyes occurs, substituting the mechanical trauma of rubbing with a soothing, diluting flush.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP review if symptoms fail to improve after 72 hours of topical therapy.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to Emergency Department or call 000 if you experience sudden loss of vision, severe unremitting eye pain, extreme sensitivity to light, or if the pupil becomes fixed and irregular.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; severe or persistent cases may require formal ophthalmologic slit-lamp evaluation.
Clinical Course: Symptoms should dramatically improve within 30 to 60 minutes of administering the topical antihistamine drops.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if the eye discharge becomes thick and yellow/green, or if your vision becomes persistently blurred.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: An allergen triggered mast cells in your eyes to release histamine, causing the severe itch and redness. The prescribed eye drops block the histamine and stop the cells from releasing more.
Actionable Steps: Do not rub your eyes. Use cold compresses to reduce swelling. Keep windows closed during high pollen days.

SMS INFORMATION

